Dear reader,

Every few years someone declares that the inverted yield curve guarantees a recession, and every few years the economy refuses to cooperate. This week I want to walk through what the curve actually measures, why it has been less reliable since the central bank balance sheet grew, and what I would look at instead: credit spreads, hiring data, and corporate earnings revisions.

None of this means a downturn cannot happen. It means one indicator should not run your portfolio. If you hold a diversified mix of stocks and bonds appropriate to your time horizon, the boring advice still applies: rebalance, keep costs low, and avoid reacting to headlines.

I read every reply. Tell me what you want covered next.

Best,
